4. CASE Expression
Definition

CASE is a standard SQL expression used to check conditions and return different results based on those conditions.

It works similarly to if-else logic in programming.

Basic Syntax
CASE
    WHEN condition THEN result
    WHEN condition THEN result
    ELSE default_result
END
Example
SELECT
    Name,
    Salary,
    CASE
        WHEN Salary >= 50000 THEN 'High Salary'
        ELSE 'Low Salary'
    END AS Salary_Category
FROM Employees;
Example Result
Name	Salary	Salary_Category
Rahul	60000	High Salary
Amit	30000	Low Salary
Important Parts
CASE → Starts the expression
WHEN → Checks a condition
THEN → Gives the result
ELSE → Gives the default result
END → Ends the expression
5. Types of CASE Expression

CASE has two main forms:

Simple CASE
Searched CASE / CASE WHEN
5.1 Simple CASE
Definition

Simple CASE is used when we want to compare one column or expression with specific values.

Syntax
CASE column_name
    WHEN value1 THEN result1
    WHEN value2 THEN result2
    ELSE default_result
END
Example

Suppose the Employees table contains different departments.

SELECT
    Name,
    Department,
    CASE Department
        WHEN 'IT' THEN 'Technical'
        WHEN 'HR' THEN 'Human Resources'
        WHEN 'Sales' THEN 'Sales Team'
        ELSE 'Other'
    END AS Department_Type
FROM Employees;
Example Result
Name	Department	Department_Type
Rahul	IT	Technical
Priya	HR	Human Resources
Amit	Sales	Sales Team
Easy Understanding

SQL compares the Department value:

IT     → Technical
HR     → Human Resources
Sales  → Sales Team

Simple CASE is useful when checking exact values.

6. Searched CASE / CASE WHEN
Definition

Searched CASE is used when we want to check conditions instead of exact values.

It can use:

Comparison operators
AND
OR
BETWEEN
IN
Syntax
CASE
    WHEN condition1 THEN result1
    WHEN condition2 THEN result2
    ELSE default_result
END
Example
SELECT
    Name,
    Salary,
    CASE
        WHEN Salary >= 70000 THEN 'High'
        WHEN Salary >= 50000 THEN 'Medium'
        ELSE 'Low'
    END AS Salary_Category
FROM Employees;
Example Result
Name	Salary	Salary_Category
Rahul	30000	Low
Priya	55000	Medium
Amit	80000	High
How SQL checks it

For salary 80000:

80000 >= 70000
       ↓
     TRUE
       ↓
     High

SQL checks conditions from top to bottom and returns the result of the first matching condition.

7. CASE with AND

We can use AND when multiple conditions must be true.

Example
SELECT
    Name,
    Department,
    Salary,
    CASE
        WHEN Department = 'IT' AND Salary > 50000
        THEN 'Senior IT Employee'
        ELSE 'Other Employee'
    END AS Employee_Type
FROM Employees;

Here both conditions must be true:

Department = 'IT'
AND
Salary > 50000
8. CASE with OR

OR is used when at least one condition should be true.

Example
SELECT
    Name,
    Department,
    CASE
        WHEN Department = 'IT'
             OR Department = 'Data'
        THEN 'Technical'
        ELSE 'Non-Technical'
    END AS Department_Type
FROM Employees;

If the department is either IT or Data, SQL returns:

Technical
9. CASE with BETWEEN

BETWEEN can be used to check a range of values.

Example
SELECT
    Student_Name,
    Marks,
    CASE
        WHEN Marks BETWEEN 75 AND 100 THEN 'Distinction'
        WHEN Marks BETWEEN 60 AND 74 THEN 'First Class'
        WHEN Marks BETWEEN 40 AND 59 THEN 'Pass'
        ELSE 'Fail'
    END AS Result
FROM Students;

This is useful for creating student result categories.

10. CASE with IN

IN can be used when checking multiple possible values.

Example
SELECT
    Name,
    Department,
    CASE
        WHEN Department IN ('IT', 'Data', 'Analytics')
        THEN 'Technical'
        ELSE 'Non-Technical'
    END AS Department_Type
FROM Employees;
11. CASE for Conditional Calculation

CASE can also be used to perform calculations.

Example

Suppose employees get different bonus percentages based on salary.

SELECT
    Name,
    Salary,
    CASE
        WHEN Salary >= 70000 THEN Salary * 0.10
        WHEN Salary >= 50000 THEN Salary * 0.05
        ELSE Salary * 0.02
    END AS Bonus
FROM Employees;

For an employee earning 80000:

80000 × 10% = 8000

So the bonus will be 8000.

12. CASE with Aggregate Functions:
CASE can be combined with functions such as SUM() and COUNT().
Example:
Count the number of IT employees:
SELECT
    SUM(
        CASE
            WHEN Department = 'IT' THEN 1
            ELSE 0
        END
    ) AS IT_Employees
FROM Employees;

If there are 5 IT employees, the result will be:

IT_Employees
5

This technique is very useful in Data Analytics and reporting.

13. IF() Function:
Definition:
IF() is a conditional function commonly available in MySQL.
It checks one condition and returns one value if the condition is true and another value if the condition is false.
IF() is database-specific. For portable SQL, CASE is generally preferred.

Syntax
IF(condition, value_if_true, value_if_false)
Example:

SELECT
    Name,
    Salary,
    IF(Salary >= 50000, 'Eligible', 'Not Eligible') AS Status
FROM Employees;

Example Result:
Name    	Salary	    Status
Rahul	    60000	      Eligible
Amit	    30000      	Not Eligible

How it works:
Salary >= 50000
       ↓
   TRUE → Eligible
   FALSE → Not Eligible

14. CASE vs IF():

CASE                                                	IF()
  
Standard SQL expression	                        Commonly used in MySQL
Can handle multiple conditions          	      Mainly handles one condition
More flexible	                                  Simple and short
Works across many SQL databases                	Database-specific
Very important for interviews                  	Useful for MySQL


CASE:
CASE
    WHEN Salary >= 50000 THEN 'Eligible'
    ELSE 'Not Eligible'
END


IF():
IF(Salary >= 50000, 'Eligible', 'Not Eligible')

15. Important Points to Remember:

A. Conditional expressions are used for decision-making in SQL.
B. CASE is the most important conditional expression.
C. Simple CASE checks exact values.
D. Searched CASE checks conditions.
E. IF() is commonly used in MySQL.
F. CASE can be used with AND, OR, BETWEEN, and IN.
G. CASE can be used with aggregate functions.
H. SQL checks WHEN conditions from top to bottom.
I. The first matching WHEN condition is returned.
J. ELSE provides the default result.
K. END is required to close a CASE expression.


